"""Authored exam distractors with directly reviewed corrections, one per entity.

The three cover-pilot records are in revisions/confidence-v2/pilot.json.
Every new entry must supply its own correction; no generic fallback is used.
"""
DISTRACTORS = []

def D(ref, claim, why):
    DISTRACTORS.append((ref, claim, why))

D('1:2.1', 'The Paigah Tombs introduce oceanic connections solely as the transport of finished goods.', 'The slide also names the movement of people and artisanal knowledge. Its intricate architectural ornament gives craft transmission a visible setting.')
D('1:3.1', 'Janamaz/Hanging identifies this textile exclusively as a prayer mat.', 'The lecture offers both terms. Its introductory role concerns materials, techniques and human interaction, so a single exclusive use would narrow the evidence.')
D('1:5.1', 'The map of the Vijayanagara Empire is a measured ground plan of the Mahanabami Dibba.', 'The maps are not a measured plan of the Mahanabami Dibba; the empire dates printed on this slide are retained as lecture assertions, not independently corrected chronology.')
D('1:7.1', 'The Krishna Temple hall carries its roof on a sequence of true arches.', 'The elephant sculptures beside the steps and the composite creatures on the pillars are different elements; the hall is not supported by true arches.')
D('1:8.1', 'The Badami Vamana is a small upright dwarf contained within a narrow frame.', 'Vamana’s narrative and the larger cosmic-stride manifestation should not be reduced to the claim that every image of Vamana is a small standing figure.')
D('1:8.2', 'The Virabhadra and Vamana images show the same deity in different historical styles.', 'These two sculptures are not the same deity in different styles.')
D('1:9.1', 'The Mahanabami Dibba’s imagery is exclusively secular, with no religious dimension.', 'The royal ceremonial and martial imagery includes Narasimha and Chenchu Lakshmi. An answer excluding the religious/cosmological dimension leaves out named figures in the programme.')
D('1:12.1', 'The stepped tank’s proximity to the Rama Temple establishes that Vasantotsava was its sole ritual use.', 'Proximity to the Rama Temple does not by itself prove a particular ritual use of the tank; the Vasantotsava connection remains interrogative.')
D('1:12.2', 'Every energetic gesture in the Rama Temple relief depicts warfare.', 'The caption includes festival activity as well as warfare. Procession, action and combat share the surface rather than identifying every gesture as fighting.')
D('1:13.1', 'The granite and green-chlorite friezes belong to one undifferentiated construction phase.', 'Slide 15 explicitly separates early granite carving from later green-chlorite facing, connecting differences in handling to the monument’s development.')
D('1:14.1', 'The sati memorial and hero stone commemorate the same kind of event.', 'A sati memorial and a hero stone are not interchangeable labels; the adjacent hero stone has several narrative registers.')
D('1:14.2', 'The Hampi hero stone is a relief cut into the Mahanabami Dibba itself.', 'The lecture compares a separate memorial stone with platform carving. Shared registers and heroic action connect the two objects without relocating the memorial onto the platform.')
D('1:15.2', 'The later green-chlorite panels are the original granite surface seen under different lighting.', 'The slide identifies later green-chlorite facing against earlier granite carving. The comparison concerns material and development phases, not a photographic lighting change.')
D('1:16.2', 'The wootz dagger supplies only a visual comparison of weapon ornament.', 'Under Trade connections, the blade stands beside the horse trader and a discussion of textiles. Its material circulation adds an economic dimension to its form.')
D('1:17.1', 'The cloud collar proves that the neighbouring Hampi performers were Chinese.', 'The later Chinese image is a comparative example, not proof that this exact model was copied at Vijayanagara.')
D('1:17.2', 'The carved performers’ costume securely identifies their nationality as Chinese.', 'Neither nationality nor a particular instrument can be securely read from these weathered figures.')
D('1:19.1', 'The Virupaksha procession image is a carved stone frieze like the Mahanabami Dibba bands.', 'A mural is not a sculpted frieze even when both use horizontal registers.')
D('1:19.2', 'The Virupaksha gopuram is the superstructure directly over the temple’s main deity.', 'Its role is a monumental entrance tower. The paired mural connects processional imagery to a temple setting, not to a claim that every tower marks a sanctuary.')
D('1:20.1', 'Chinnamadevi’s joined hands depict the same command gesture as an armed royal warrior.', 'Her devotional posture is part of a royal bronze group. The political identity of a queen coexists with the religious act represented.')
D('1:20.2', 'Krishnadevaraya’s bronze represents an anonymous worshipper with no political significance.', 'The named king’s devotional posture joins royal identity to sacred representation; removing the political dimension loses half of that relationship.')
D('1:20.3', 'Tirumaladevi’s bronze is a duplicate view of Chinnamadevi rather than a distinct figure.', 'The group contains separately identified bronzes of Tirumaladevi, Krishnadevaraya and Chinnamadevi. The shared pose and setting connect distinct royal figures.')
D('1:20.4', 'The Lepakshi kabai and kullayi prove that the wearers converted to Islam.', 'The robe and cap participate in an adopted courtly dress vocabulary. A clothing form connects political presentation and cultural exchange; it does not establish conversion.')
D('1:21.1', 'The Vijaya Vitthala frieze image shows the Mahanabami Dibba’s royal platform.', 'The caption locates these friezes at Vijaya Vitthala Temple. The module compares their interregional imagery with the royal platform while retaining the separate architectural sites.')
D('1:21.2', 'The composite animals on the Vijaya Vitthala columns are decorative pictures unrelated to the supports.', 'The animal forms project from the carved columns within a repeated structural bay system; ornament and support are encountered together.')
D('1:22.1', 'The three Vijaya Vitthala warrior panels are repeated views of one carved figure.', 'The three panels contain different warrior figures, costumes and accompanying forms. Their juxtaposition is a comparison within a shared temple context.')
D('1:22.2', 'The central warrior’s composite companion is an ordinary horse.', 'The creature combines animal components, including a curling trunk; that hybrid form differs from an ordinary mounted-warrior animal.')
D('1:22.3', 'A foreign-looking hat on the right warrior securely identifies a particular European nationality.', 'Costume comparisons support the lecture’s interregional reading; they do not prove the biography of a real sitter.')
D('1:23.1', 'Lotus Mahal’s arches establish that every part of its architecture follows a single arcuate vocabulary.', 'Cusped arches coexist with horizontal eaves and tiered roof forms. The lecture’s argument concerns a combination of architectural vocabularies within one courtly building.')
D('1:24.2', 'The foreground arch framing Charminar is one of Charminar’s four corner minarets.', 'The large foreground arch in Module 3 slide 21 is Char Kaman, not the whole Charminar.')
D('oceanic-networks-as-an-art-historical-method', 'The course defines oceanic networks as the movement of commodities without people or techniques.', 'An inventory of imports is incomplete: the lecture also emphasizes craft decisions, people and changing meanings.')
D('2:1.1', 'The full palampore and its enlarged detail are two separate textiles.', 'The enlarged image selects a portion of the same hanging, accession 934.4.3. Overall floral organization and the smaller hunting scene are read at different scales.')
D('2:2.1', 'The historical Coromandel map and the adjacent route diagram were printed together in 1753.', 'The red lecture labels are not necessarily original eighteenth-century printing; the two maps also have different dates and functions.')
D('2:3.1', 'The uncaptioned riverside temple landscape identifies a particular kalamkari workshop.', 'The nearby Srikalahasti caption on the following slide does not by itself identify this uncaptioned site.')
D('2:4.1', 'The Srikalahasti artisan repeats the design by stamping a carved block.', 'The visible hand drawing is the point of comparison with the next slide’s block-printing demonstration. Both contribute to the technique discussion through different actions.')
D('2:5.1', 'The Polavaram demonstration uses the same freehand drawing action as the Srikalahasti photograph.', 'The Polavaram artisan presses a block onto cloth; the Srikalahasti artisan draws contours. Repeating a prepared matrix and drawing a line are distinct visible actions.')
D('2:5.2', 'The number of colours visible in the hanging equals the number of printing blocks used.', 'Visible colours are the result of the making sequence. Overlap, reserves and dye stages prevent a one-colour-to-one-block inference from the finished hanging.')
D('2:6.1', 'The full five-panel tent and the Gandaberunda detail are different objects.', 'Slide 7 enlarges a bird panel from the five-panel textile on slide 6. The detail supports close motif comparison without creating a second object identity.')
D('2:7.1', 'The Manga malai necklace is a fragment cut from the painted tent panel.', 'The necklace is a three-dimensional jewellery object with coloured insets and pendant elements. Its two-headed bird is compared with the painted tent motif across different materials.')
D('2:7.3', 'The double-headed bird has an identical religious meaning on the Dutch gravestone and Deccani tent.', 'The slide calls this a two-headed bird; it does not prove that the Dutch heraldic emblem has the same religious meaning as Gandaberunda.')
D('2:8.1', 'The Lepakshi procession and Golconda hanging are painted on the same kind of support.', 'Lepakshi is a wall/ceiling painting and the Golconda hanging is cloth. Processional figures and costume connect their imagery across those different supports.')
D('2:8.2', 'Calico Museum 647 and National Museum 48.7-103 identify the same Golconda textile.', 'Hanging and coverlet are not interchangeable object identities; the two Golconda textiles have different collections and formats.')
D('2:9.1', 'The Bijapur mihrab is an entrance doorway through which worshippers pass.', 'A mihrab is not a gateway through which worshippers enter.')
D('2:9.2', 'The gateway’s architectural ornament establishes that the kalamkari works were made from its building materials.', 'The comparison follows framing, vegetal and animal ornament between architecture and textile. It does not transfer masonry materials into the cloth-making process.')
D('2:10.1', 'The Dutch warehouse identifies Dutch workers as the makers of all textiles traded through it.', 'The warehouse represents commercial infrastructure. Trading, collecting and physically making a textile are distinct roles; the lecture’s production demonstrations identify craft activity separately.')
D('2:11.1', 'The Golconda coverlet’s pavilion scene is another episode of the Ramayana textile.', 'The coverlet centres on a courtly pavilion with figures, animals and plants. The separately identified Ramayana textile organizes a devotional narrative in framed episodes.')
D('2:12.1', 'The Ramayana textile is significant only as a traded cotton commodity.', 'Its narrative registers and central devotional grouping supply a religious dimension alongside technique and circulation; the subject is integral to its organization.')
D('2:13.2', 'The Manikkavachar–Meenakshi hanging and Golconda coverlet have the same narrative subject.', 'Shared kalamkari practice does not imply identical themes, dates or viewing formats.')
D('2:14.2', 'The pineapple comparison means the tomb doorway and textile had the same original function.', 'The doorway belongs to a tomb and the cloth belongs to a textile context. The pineapple connects a travelling motif across different objects and uses.')
D('2:15.1', 'The dodot was manufactured in Java because Javanese court elites wore it.', 'A textile used in Java is not necessarily manufactured there.')
D('2:16.1', 'The chintz petticoat was manufactured entirely in Europe because it forms part of European dress.', 'Indian fabric and European garment construction are separate aspects of origin; neither cancels the other.')
D('2:16.2', 'The chintz jacket and petticoat are interchangeable views of one garment.', 'The jacket and petticoat are separate upper and lower garments. Coordinated chintz links them as an ensemble while preserving their distinct cuts and functions.')
D('domingo-paes-on-vijayanagaras-urban-abundance', 'Paes’s comparison with Rome is a measured calculation of Vijayanagara’s area.', 'The traveller’s comparison with Rome is a situated report, not a measured calculation of city area.')
D('coromandel-textile-networks-and-changing-uses', 'The module explains Coromandel textiles only through their European consumers.', 'A trade account limited to Europe omits the explicit Southeast Asian example.')
D('3:2.1', 'Siesta’s Persianate setting establishes that the painting was manufactured in Iran.', 'The lecture locates Siesta in Bijapur. Persianate names a connected visual and courtly culture; it does not replace that local production context with Iranian manufacture.')
D('3:3.1', 'The resemblance to Timurid madrasas makes Mahmud Gawan Madrasa a Central Asian building rather than a Deccani one.', 'Mahmud Gawan Madrasa stands at Bidar. The Central Asian comparison addresses related architectural vocabulary adopted in the Deccan, not the building’s geographical relocation.')
D('3:4.2', 'Every building visible in the Registan photograph was constructed by Ulugh Beg.', 'The photograph shows a multi-building ensemble. The lecture names Ulugh Beg Madrasa within it, so that attribution cannot be extended to every visible building.')
D('3:5.1', 'The Bidar well image shows the complete hydraulic plan of the palace gardens.', 'The visible well is not itself a complete chahar-bagh plan; the slide connects water infrastructure and garden planning at a broader scale.')
D('3:5.2', 'The Bahmani basin is a domed religious building like the nearby Sola Khamba Masjid.', 'The basin is a water feature shown beside other components of Bidar’s landscape. The mosque is a separate building with a different function in the same spatial discussion.')
D('3:5.3', 'The Sola Khamba Masjid photograph is a full ground plan locating every column.', 'Do not infer a complete mosque plan or count of supports merely from the building’s name.')
D('3:6.1', 'Bidri’s bright decoration is ordinary white paint applied to an engraved iron surface.', 'Inlay inserts a contrasting material; engraving cuts the surface. They are not synonyms.')
D('3:6.2', 'Ghalamzani and kalamkari name the same dyeing process on different objects.', 'Ghalamzani concerns working a metal surface; kalamkari concerns patterned cloth and dye processes. A related tool-word does not make the materials or procedures identical.')
D('3:7.1', 'The turbaned miniature detail is a securely identified self-portrait of Farrukh Beg.', 'A video thumbnail without a caption cannot be assigned to Farrukh Beg merely because his portrait appears on the next slide.')
D('3:8.1', 'The self-portrait’s publication in a Met catalogue establishes the Met as its owner.', 'The catalogue calls it a presumed self-portrait in the Seitz collection. Publishing institution, collection and qualified subject identification are separate facts.')
D('3:9.1', 'The image of Ibrahim Adil Shah riding Atash Khan has only an aesthetic function, without courtly political significance.', 'The ruler is distinguished by position, dress and the elephant’s elaborate trappings. Course reading: these choices represent courtly status, giving the image a political as well as visual/aesthetic dimension.')
D('3:9.2', 'The Sarasvati painting proves that Ibrahim Adil Shah II converted to Hinduism.', 'The pairing connects Sarasvati’s devotional and musical associations with Bijapuri court culture. It establishes that pictorial relationship, not a conversion by the patron.')
D('3:10.1', 'The Kitab-e-Nauras page and Sangeet Mahal are evidence only of architectural patronage.', 'The page introduces literary and musical activity beside a building. The grouping makes patronage operate across text, music, imagery and architecture.')
D('3:10.2', 'Sangeet Mahal can be understood as an isolated ruin with no connection to the adjacent musical text.', 'The lecture pairs the building with Kitab-e-Nauras and follows Sarasvati and royal imagery, making musical patronage the connecting argument.')
D('3:11.1', 'The two European prints above Ibrahim’s tambur portrait are parts of the original painted album page.', 'The European prints and tambur portrait are separate images arranged on the lecture slide. Their pedagogical juxtaposition must be distinguished from an artwork’s original album mounting.')
D('3:11.2', 'The left European print’s placement proves that it was the direct model for the tambur portrait.', 'The slide places the print in comparison with the tambur portrait under a travelling-image discussion. Its arrangement supports comparison without documenting an exact source-copy chain.')
D('3:11.3', 'The right European print establishes the sitter in Ibrahim’s portrait as a European musician.', 'The portrait names Ibrahim Adil Shah II. The neighbouring European print is a comparative image, so its subject does not replace the identity of the painted ruler.')
D('3:12.1', 'The mynah and jewellery settle the Yogini’s identity as Andal.', 'Andal, royalty and a Chinese-porcelain source for foliage are questions in the slide, not settled attributions.')
D('3:13.1', 'The modern marbling video documents the exact workshop recipe used to make Ascetic Riding a Nag.', 'The contemporary tutorial illustrates a principle; its recipe cannot be projected unchanged into early modern practice.')
D('3:13.2', 'The nag in Ascetic Riding a Nag is a serpent deity depicted as the rider’s mount.', 'Nag here means an old or worn horse, not a naga serpent.')
D('3:14.1', 'The comparison rider is another photograph of Ascetic Riding a Nag rather than a separate image.', 'The two images are separate objects or versions, not merely a colour-adjusted crop of one work.')
D('3:15.1', 'Bijapur’s rounded perimeter proves direct copying of a Kakatiya city plan.', 'Reference to Kakatiyas is a question; resemblance of rounded plans does not prove direct derivation.')
D('3:16.1', 'Gol Gumbaz’s corner towers are the pendentives that span its central room.', 'The corner towers are vertical elements outside the central volume. The diagram treats the spanning and supporting system of the dome separately from those towers.')
D('3:17.1', 'The uncaptioned domed complex is another view of Gol Gumbaz with the same massing.', 'The uncaptioned view shows a different multi-part domed complex. Its provisional identification remains in the attribution note; it is not merged into the distinctive Gol Gumbaz mass.')
D('3:18.1', 'The dagger hilt depicts one ordinary animal, so its significance is purely practical grip design.', 'The hilt combines a curling trunk, bird-like forms, openwork and coloured eyes. Its composite imagery and worked surface give it decorative and courtly significance beyond gripping the blade.')
D('3:19.1', 'The portrait and zoomorphic dagger hilt are unrelated because one depicts a ruler and the other an animal.', 'The lecture explicitly asks about their relationship: courtly representation and decorated elite objects connect status, material culture and imagery across media.')
D('3:20.1', 'House of Bijapur documents a single gathering attended by every ruler portrayed.', 'The painting constructs a dynastic grouping across generations. Its political account of lineage does not require every ruler to have attended one simultaneous gathering.')
D('3:21.1', 'The 1854 Hyderabad map is the city’s original foundation plan and proves four intact gardens.', 'An 1854 map records the city at that time; it is not automatically the original foundation plan or proof of four intact gardens.')
D('3:22.1', 'Nicolaas Witsen painted Khawwas-Khan’s portrait because it appears in his album.', 'Witsen is the collector in the lecture’s account. The courtier, painter and later collector have different roles in the portrait’s history.')
D('3:23.1', 'A Christian subject makes the Golconda Adoration a European-manufactured painting.', 'Christian subject matter does not make this a European-made painting or prove conversion of its patron.')
D('3:23.2', 'The Badshahi Ashurkhana is a generic mosque whose function is fully explained by its arch shape.', 'The lecture identifies an ashurkhana, a commemorative mourning setting. The building’s ritual function adds information that a description of its arches alone would omit.')
D('3:24.1', 'Collaert’s bird engraving is a Deccani miniature because it appears beside Deccani paintings.', 'The lecture identifies an Antwerp engraving by Collaert. Its placement beside Deccani paintings creates a cross-media comparison rather than changing its maker or process.')
D('3:24.2', 'The tethered ram is an incidental background detail with no compositional role.', 'The ram anchors the lower part of the image while the larger parrot occupies the tree above. Their unequal scales and vertical placement organize the composition.')
D('3:24.3', 'The dervish gathering is a natural-history study equivalent in function to Collaert’s bird print.', 'The human gathering and attending animals form a social setting. The slide’s cross-image animal comparison leaves the subjects and functions distinct.')
D('persianate-cosmopolis', 'Persianate describes an object’s manufacture in Iran, regardless of where its makers worked.', 'A Persianate object can be made in the Deccan by locally active artisans; the term is not a provenance label.')
D('4:1.1', 'Vosmaer’s bouquet records one naturally occurring group of flowers exactly as gathered.', 'This is a painting of flowers, not a botanical inventory proving that all species bloomed together or were imported by the same route.')
D('4:2.1', 'Vermeer’s globe makes the Geographer an imperial conqueror in the same sense as Jahangir standing on a globe.', 'The globe belongs to an interior of geographic study; its presence alone does not carry the same imperial allegory as Jahangir standing on a globe.')
D('4:4.1', 'The video’s VOC ship model is the historical trading ship itself.', 'The image documents a model; it is not a photograph of a seventeenth-century ship at sea.')
D('4:5.1', 'Still Life with Lobster and Fruit is significant only for decorative abundance, with no economic dimension.', 'The lavish foods and vessels make abundance visible. Course reading: the lecture places that display within materiality and trade networks, so economic circulation accompanies the visual/aesthetic dimension.')
D('4:6.1', 'The convex mirror shows an empty reflection and contributes nothing beyond the central weighing scene.', 'The lower-right convex mirror reflects a street and two people. It introduces an exterior view within the shop scene rather than repeating an empty surface.')
D('4:7.1', 'Dou’s timepiece establishes the exact time when the still life was painted.', 'The timepiece is a represented crafted object, not a timestamp for making the painting. Course reading: it joins an attention to time with the labour invested in objects and their depiction.')
D('4:8.1', 'Van Aelst’s armour still life documents an actual battle in progress.', 'The armour is arranged as an object within a still life. Its represented surfaces and surrounding possessions establish a display, rather than the action of a battle.')
D('4:9.1', 'The peacocks and partridges illustrate the same treatment of birds as dead game.', 'The peacocks occupy a living garden scene; the partridges on the following slide are dead game in an arranged still life. The comparison changes the birds’ condition and pictorial use.')
D('4:10.1', 'The partridges are living ornamental birds in a garden like the peacocks on the previous slide.', 'The bird is dead game in a still life; its spread wing is not evidence of flight or a living wildlife study.')
D('ocean-at-the-table-commodities-and-still-life', 'An oceanic reading requires every object in every still life to be an imported commodity.', 'A trade-based reading does not require every painted object to be imported.')
D('pigment-preparation-v-a-video-still', 'The green-blue colour in the preparation video identifies a specific mineral pigment and recipe.', 'Green-blue appearance alone cannot identify malachite, azurite or a chemical recipe.')
D('5:2.1', 'The Mughal and Iranian animal carpets share one confirmed manufacturing origin.', 'Similar animal-and-flower vocabulary does not establish that the carpets came from one workshop or use identical materials.')
D('5:2.2', 'The Iranian carpet’s animal motifs make it identical in function and manufacture to the Mughal example.', 'Similar animal-and-flower vocabulary does not establish that the carpets came from one workshop or use identical materials.')
D('5:3.1', 'The revived Dhaka muslin sari is an unaltered historical garment from the period of Mughal trade.', 'These modern illustrations are not securely dated surviving Mughal garments; do not transfer the lecture’s historical frame into an object date.')
D('5:3.2', 'The transparency demonstration independently proves an exact historical thread count.', 'The image demonstrates translucency through cloth. An exact historical thread count would require a measurement and attribution separate from that visible effect.')
D('5:4.1', 'Akbar’s clothing in the Adham Khan scene is relevant only to decoration, not to legitimacy.', 'The slide’s heading connects sartorial choices with legitimacy. Course reading: dress distinguishes the ruler within a political narrative, so treating it as decoration alone omits its social and political work.')
D('5:5.1', 'The displayed nineteenth-century copy after Luini was Basawan’s direct source in about 1590.', 'The nineteenth-century painting after Luini on slide 6 cannot be the direct source for a painting dated around 1590.')
D('5:6.1', 'A shared Virgin-and-Child subject makes the Luini copy and Basawan image identical in composition.', 'The shared mother-and-child subject supports comparison, while setting, figural handling and surrounding details differ. The displayed work after Luini is also a separately dated object.')
D('5:7.1', 'Akbar’s combat-of-ascetics scene uses the same quiet, contained figure grouping as Virgin of the Rocks.', 'A scene with ascetics is not necessarily a devotional image; here the subject is armed conflict viewed within a court narrative.')
D('5:8.1', 'A dark background alone establishes sfumato in Virgin of the Rocks.', 'Sfumato describes soft tonal transition; it is not synonymous with single-point linear perspective or with all European painting.')
D('5:9.1', 'Every visible part of Akbar’s Church belongs to one original construction phase.', 'The lecture’s 1580s date does not prove that the photographed neoclassical-looking porch survives unchanged from that decade.')
D('5:10.1', 'The Mughal Deposition is a mechanical reproduction that contributes no compositional alteration.', 'Derivative identifies a source relationship, not a lack of invention.')
D('5:11.1', 'The Christian-imagery canteen is a Mughal object because it appears under the early-modern-empires heading.', 'This mid-thirteenth-century object predates the Safavid and Mughal empires named in the slide heading; it serves as comparative background.')
D('5:11.2', 'The Birth of the Prophet Muhammad is a Mughal painting contemporary with the surrounding Akbar examples.', 'This is an Ilkhanid manuscript image around 1314, not a Mughal image of the Virgin and Child despite its placement in a comparative slide.')
D('5:12.1', 'Ghulam’s Virgin and Child supplies evidence of the maker’s Christian conversion.', 'The Christian subject participates in the court’s collection and adaptation of diverse visual resources. Subject choice does not document the maker’s religious affiliation.')
D('5:13.1', 'The Bihzad and Abd al-Samad Camel Fights are two reproductions of the same sheet.', 'These are separate works, not two reproductions of one painting.')
D('5:13.2', 'Silsila in the Camel Fight comparison means the two painters attended the same physical scene.', 'Silsila frames a relationship of artistic transmission and emulation between separate works. It does not require the painters to have witnessed one simultaneous encounter.')
D('5:15.1', 'The queried Dürer attribution is a confirmed signature read from this slide.', 'The question mark in the lecture’s attribution must remain until an independent catalogue confirms this print.')
D('5:15.2', 'Abu’l Hasan’s Saint John is a European woodblock impression made by Dürer himself.', 'The drawing is not an independent eyewitness depiction of Saint John or a European print impression.')
D('5:16.1', 'The hawking scene is documentary evidence that all figures in the lineage discussion met together.', 'The image represents Abdullah Khan hawking within a courtly scene. The sequence’s lineage argument concerns relationships among images and practices, not a documented gathering of every figure discussed.')
D('5:17.2', 'Raimondi’s Descent from the Cross is a unique painted manuscript page like the Mughal adaptation.', 'Raimondi’s work is an engraving after Raphael’s design. It is a reproducible print used in comparison with the separately painted Mughal Deposition.')
D('5:18.1', 'The Gulshan composite page was created in one medium by one artist in one sitting.', 'One assembled folio need not have one artist or production date; dates of component images and assembly differ.')
D('5:18.2', 'Luna’s European print date dates every Persian drawing and border on the composite page.', 'Luna belongs to the small printed component, not to every figure on the composite folio.')
D('5:18.3', 'The seated-man drawing is a figure printed from the same plate as Luna.', 'The seated man is a separate drawing inset mounted into the composite sheet. Luna is a distinct printed component, so the common page does not make them one plate impression.')
D('5:18.4', 'The three-men drawing proves that the whole Gulshan page depicts one shared narrative event.', 'The three-men drawing is one component among separately assembled images. The album’s mounting creates an association rather than a continuous scene shared by all components.')
D('5:18.5', 'The reclining lion belongs inside Luna’s original planetary composition.', 'The lion is a separately framed drawing on the assembled folio. It belongs to the album’s new juxtaposition, not to the original planetary engraving.')
D('5:19.1', 'Jahangir’s arrow proves that he personally defeated and beheaded Malik Ambar.', 'This is a symbolic victory image, not evidence that Jahangir personally shot Malik Anbar.')
D('5:21.1', 'Jahangir’s embrace of Shah Abbas represents an equal alliance through equal figure scale.', 'Jahangir is larger and occupies the privileged imperial position. Course reading: the embrace, globe and animal pairing idealize a hierarchy rather than representing equal sovereign scale.')
D('5:22.1', 'Hashim painted both Jahangir and Jesus because the images share one folio.', 'The common accession 07A.12 belongs to the shared folio; it does not justify merging Jahangir and Jesus into one depicted person or one artist’s work.')
D('5:22.2', 'The shared folio proves that Abu’l Hasan painted the emperor as a Christian ruler.', 'Jahangir and Jesus are distinct subjects with separate artist credits on a shared folio. Their mounting connects images without converting the imperial portrait into a depiction of a Christian ruler.')
D('5:23.1', 'Shah Jahan’s angels and halo make the folio a Christian devotional image rather than imperial self-representation.', 'The halo and angels are adapted visual resources, not evidence that the subject is a Christian saint.')
D('5:24.2', 'The standing-ruler drawing and Chitarman’s folio are parts of one Mughal manuscript sheet.', 'The drawing and Chitarman folio are separate images placed in comparison on the lecture slide. Their juxtaposition discusses the reception of Mughal ruler imagery across different makers and media.')
D('globe-in-the-mughal-court', 'Imported European imagery was copied passively by the Mughal court without adaptation.', 'Circulation does not imply passive copying or uniform adoption of European naturalism.')
D('occidentalism-and-orientalism-lecture-comparison', 'Occidentalism reverses Orientalism with exactly equivalent political power relations.', 'Do not assume Occidentalism is an exact political mirror of Orientalism, or that a borrowed European motif proves colonial domination of its Mughal user.')
D('6:1.1', 'The Calcutta Art Studio Buddha has a European religious subject because lithography originated in Europe.', 'A European origin for the print technology does not make the religious subject European.')
D('6:2.1', 'Kernot made every stage of Fort at Agra, including the original sketch and intermediate drawing.', 'The credits name Bacon for the sketch, Stanfield for the drawing and Kernot for engraving. The print therefore records a division of image-making labour.')
D('6:3.1', 'The unfinished Pedana woodblock is itself a finished printed textile.', 'A woodblock is the printing matrix, not the finished cloth or a paper print.')
D('6:3.2', 'The Polavaram cotton uses the same paper-print support as the Saint Sebastian impression.', 'The Polavaram object is printed cotton, while the Saint Sebastian impression is on paper. A shared relief-printing principle does not make their supports identical.')
D('6:4.1', 'The cut-away recesses of the Saint Sebastian woodblock carry the printing ink.', 'Relief printing inks the raised portions of the block; the cut-away areas form unprinted spaces. Recessed ink-bearing lines belong to an intaglio principle.')
D('6:4.2', 'The Saint Sebastian impression is the carved matrix rather than the transferred image.', 'The impression is the image transferred from the matrix onto paper. The carved block is the separate object paired with it on the slide.')
D('6:5.1', 'The lithographic stone prints by raised relief in exactly the same way as the Saint Sebastian block.', 'A stone with an image is not evidence of relief carving; lithography separates image and non-image areas chemically.')
D('6:6.1', 'The relief-printing demonstration shows acid biting recessed lines into a metal plate.', 'The left photograph demonstrates relief printing; the right diagram on the same slide explains a different, planographic process.')
D('6:7.1', 'The name Hodges gives him sole credit for both drawing and engraving Sicri Gully.', 'The lecture credits Hodges and Angus in different stages of the image’s production. The drawing source and engraved realization must retain their separate maker roles.')
D('6:7.2', 'The Salsette temple view is a measured architectural survey unaffected by composition.', 'The print presents an arranged viewpoint with spatial framing and selected figures and terrain. It supports a picturesque reading rather than a claim to a complete measured plan.')
D('6:11.1', 'The Kanaree cave print was drawn at the time the ancient caves were constructed.', 'The print’s date is not the date of the landscape or ancient architecture it depicts.')
D('6:8.1', 'The title page’s architectural tablet is an inscription physically found at an Indian monument.', 'The tablet is a pictorial device carrying the publication’s title and dedication. Architectural fragments frame that printed title rather than locating a discovered monument inscription.')
D('6:9.1', 'Koummars is an unmediated photograph of potters rather than a constructed published image.', 'Koummars is a published representation organized around an occupational subject. Its maker selects pose and arrangement; it is not a photographic capture.')
D('6:10.1', 'A Bazar preserves the entire social reality of an Indian market without selection or framing.', 'The print records an authored view of a market, not an exhaustive inventory of Calcutta commerce.')
D('6:12.1', 'Roxburgh’s name establishes that he personally drew, engraved and coloured the botanical plate.', 'Roxburgh is associated with commissioning botanical drawings, while Indian artists contributed the images. Individual drawing, engraving and colouring credits for this plate are separate attribution questions.')
D('6:13.1', 'The broom scene represents a named historical scandal solely because it is a Kalighat painting.', 'The visible subject is a woman striking a man with a broom. Kalighat’s capacity for social satire does not by itself attach this scene to the separately named Mahant–Elokeshi narrative.')
D('6:13.2', 'The Kalighat cat and the Battala cat were both printed from the same woodblock.', 'The Kalighat example is painted and the Battala example is printed. A shared cat-and-lobster subject connects their imagery without giving them a common printing matrix.')
D('6:14.1', 'The Battala cat is the original hand-painted Kalighat sheet reproduced on the previous slide.', 'The shared subject does not make the Kalighat painting and Battala woodcut the same object.')
D('6:15.1', 'Shiva’s veena makes this Battala image a documentary portrait of an ordinary professional musician.', 'The caption identifies Shiva, and the veena belongs to that devotional image. A represented musical action does not turn the deity into an ordinary documentary sitter.')
D('6:16.1', 'The Mahant–Elokeshi encounter and raised-blade image depict the same instant in the narrative.', 'The two scenes are distinct images in a narrative sequence, not duplicate reproductions of one action.')
D('6:16.2', 'The raised-blade image is a second view of the earlier temple encounter, with no narrative development.', 'The two scenes are distinct images in a narrative sequence, not duplicate reproductions of one action.')
D('6:16.3', 'Durga’s family print is another illustration of the Mahant–Elokeshi scandal.', 'The named goddess and attendant family form a devotional ensemble. The Mahant–Elokeshi images alongside it recount a different social narrative.')
D('6:17.1', 'The lithographed cover is a manuscript original written separately for every purchaser.', 'The cover is identified as lithographed: writing and ornament are reproduced through printing. It is not a separately handwritten original for each owner.')
D('6:17.2', 'Tiger (Tugra) is a natural-history engraving whose letterforms play no part in its animal outline.', 'The tiger’s animal outline is built from calligraphic forms. Text and shape work together, rather than serving as a zoological engraving independent of writing.')
D('6:17.3', 'The hawk’s chromolithographic process and the tiger’s lithographic label identify the same colour-printing specification.', 'The hawk is labelled chromolithograph, specifying colour lithographic production; the tiger has the broader lithograph label. Neither label supplies a counted number of matrices.')
D('6:18.1', 'Hogarth’s publication credit makes him the original sketcher and engraver of Scene in Bombay.', 'The credits distinguish Grindlay’s source, Reeve’s printmaking role and Hogarth’s publishing role. Publishing an impression is different from drawing and engraving it.')
D('6:18.2', 'Bagchi’s Vijaya shows that Indian artists contributed subject matter but no agency to the print enterprise.', 'Bagchi is a named artist within the image-making enterprise. Course reading: indigenous artistic and studio agency accompanies the adoption of print technology; it is not confined to supplying subjects.')
D('6:19.1', 'Kali and Tara are identical repetitions of one goddess made by hand-colouring a monochrome sheet.', 'Kali and Tara are not interchangeable labels for one repeated figure; compare attributes, garlands, ground and body treatment.')
D('6:21.1', 'The title Mahavir allows this print to be treated as another Calcutta Art Studio image of the Buddha.', 'The lecture gives Mahavir and Chitrasala Studio, whereas the Buddha image is credited to Calcutta Art Studio. Subject and producer remain separate, with the exact Mahavir identification qualified in the attribution note.')
D('relief-intaglio-and-planography', 'Lithography belongs to relief printing because its demonstration shows marks raised above the drawing surface.', 'The stylized diagram’s protruding greasy marks do not make lithography a relief process.')
D('chromolithography-and-hand-colouring', 'Any coloured print is a chromolithograph, including one coloured by hand after printing.', 'Chromolithography prints colour through lithographic operations. Hand-colouring adds colour after an impression is printed; the two processes can also be combined in one object.')
D('misregistration-analytical-study-term', 'Mention of misregistration proves that the Kali/Tara impression in the lecture contains a printing defect.', 'Do not turn this general technical concept into a claim that a particular lecture print is defective without inspecting sufficient evidence.')
D('european-print-and-indian-bazaar', 'European printing replaced an image-less Indian market with no indigenous printing or painting practices.', 'A single story of European invention replacing an image-less India ignores the indigenous textile-printing and painting examples placed early in the module.')
D('7:1.1', 'The Great Wave is a European oil painting because it influenced European painters.', 'The Great Wave is a Japanese woodblock print. Its later influence on European painting concerns reception, not a change in the source object’s medium.')
D('7:2.1', 'The ukiyo-e video thumbnail is itself an independently catalogued original impression.', 'The phrase Introduction to Ukiyo-e titles the video; it is not a title or secure medium identification for the reproduced artwork.')
D('7:3.1', 'Lake Chuzenji and The Great Wave are two titles for the same Japanese landscape print.', 'Lake Chuzenji is a separate landscape organized through a foreground frame of trees. The Great Wave instead juxtaposes a dominant wave, boats and distant Fuji.')
D('7:4.1', 'Eisen’s courtesan was copied from Van Gogh’s later painted courtesan.', 'Eisen’s print is not Van Gogh’s painting, even where the figure is closely copied.')
D('7:4.2', 'Van Gogh’s courtesan is a Japanese woodblock print because it follows Eisen’s design.', 'Derivative describes the acknowledged source relationship; the painted border and changed palette show active transformation.')
D('7:5.1', 'The Japanese images behind Père Tanguy establish that the portrait was painted in a Japanese room observed on location.', 'The background expresses an artist’s collected and imagined Japan; it does not depict a Japanese room observed on location.')
D('7:6.1', 'Sunflowers reproduces one identified Japanese print rather than adapting a broader pictorial vocabulary.', 'The lecture connects colour, contour and plant forms with Van Gogh’s imagined Japan. It does not identify a single Japanese print as the direct source of this Sunflowers composition.')
D('7:6.2', 'The Arles 1888 heading supplies the production date of Almond Blossoms.', 'The heading’s Arles 1888 date does not date Almond Blossoms, which the caption gives as 1890.')
D('7:7.1', 'Zola’s portrait depicts the writer without any references to collecting or artistic association.', 'Books, the Manet booklet, the screen and the pictures on the wall construct Zola’s artistic and intellectual associations. The enlargements make those relationships part of the portrait’s reading.')
D('7:7.2', 'The small Olympia in Zola’s portrait is the separately exhibited original Olympia canvas.', 'Do not treat the painted depiction as an independently inspected physical print or use it to establish an edition.')
D('7:7.3', 'The Japanese figure in Zola’s portrait is a physical print whose precise edition can be read from the slide.', 'Do not treat the painted depiction as an independently inspected physical print or use it to establish an edition.')
D('7:7.4', 'The partly obscured monochrome reproduction has the same evidential status as a fully inspected original print.', 'Do not treat the painted depiction as an independently inspected physical print or use it to establish an edition.')
D('7:8.1', 'The Art Institute Water Lilies and the wide MoMA work are two views of the same painting.', 'The Art Institute painting and MoMA’s wide multi-panel work are separate works sharing a series title.')
D('7:8.2', 'The MoMA Water Lilies inherits the date and physical dimensions of the smaller Art Institute painting.', 'Do not apply the 1897–99 date of the left-hand Art Institute example to the undated MoMA work.')
D('7:9.1', 'Jane Avril’s Japanese-inspired cropping makes the performance a Japanese subject and the medium a woodblock print.', 'Japanese-inspired composition does not change the subject into a Japanese performance or make the process Japanese woodblock printing.')
D('7:9.2', 'Divan Japonais has no commercial function because its cropped silhouettes are discussed as aesthetic devices.', 'Divan Japonais advertises a venue and performance culture. Course reading: its cropping and silhouettes serve a commercial poster, so aesthetic and economic functions coexist.')
D('7:10.1', 'La Japonaise documents an anonymous Japanese woman in her everyday surroundings.', 'The wearer is Camille Monet, not an anonymous Japanese woman.')
D('7:10.2', 'Cassatt’s The Letter is a woodblock directly derived from Mizuno’s later After the Bath.', 'The finished Letter uses intaglio processes, including aquatint, rather than woodblock. Its 1890–91 date also precedes the 1893 Mizuno print, making that pairing parallel rather than a direct source relationship.')
D('7:10.3', 'The Kansei era in After the Bath’s title is the date when this impression was designed.', 'The Kansei era in the subject title is not the print’s production date.')
D('7:11.1', 'The paired bridge motif proves that Monet directly copied this exact Hokusai sheet.', 'A bridge-shaped curve in two images establishes a comparison, not proof Monet copied this exact sheet.')
D('7:11.2', 'Monet’s bridge serves the same urban transport role as Hokusai’s populated Mannen Bridge.', 'Monet’s bridge has a different scale, setting and function from the populated urban bridge in Hokusai’s print.')
D('7:12.1', 'Monet’s sitter label also identifies him as the photographer of this portrait.', 'The overlaid label identifies the sitter; it does not supply the photograph’s maker or date.')
D('7:12.2', 'The film-strip border dates Cassatt’s portrait to the production of the lecture video.', 'The film-strip and tape graphics belong to the lecture video presentation. They frame an older photographic portrait rather than dating its original exposure.')
D('7:13.1', 'Beato’s applied colour makes Geisha’s Dancing a chromolithograph rather than a photograph.', 'The object is a hand-coloured albumen photograph. Colour added to a photographic image is different from printing a chromolithograph from colour lithographic matrices.')
D('7:14.1', 'The Tokyo view’s low roofs establish an unchanging, wholly premodern city.', 'A view of low roofs is not proof of an unchanging or wholly premodern Japan.')
D('7:14.2', 'Yoshikazu’s French is a photograph produced by the camera represented within it.', 'This is a print depicting photography, not a photograph taken by the man it represents.')
D('moma-water-lilies-gallery-arrangement', 'The gallery seating and low barrier are painted elements within Monet’s Water Lilies.', 'Display furniture and barriers are part of the museum installation, not Monet’s painted composition.')
D('japonisme-appreciation-collecting-and-appropriation', 'Japonisme requires European artists to adopt Japanese woodblock manufacture rather than adapt pictorial devices.', 'Van Gogh and Monet adapt Japanese pictorial resources in painting, while Cassatt uses intaglio. Japonisme concerns reception and transformation, including cases where the European artist retains a different medium.')
D('8:1.1', 'The Ahmedabad poster’s exhibition dates give the manufacture dates of every textile pictured.', 'The poster’s exhibition dates are not the dates of every pictured textile.')
D('8:1.2', 'The floral poster crop identifies its cloth as a complete, securely dated kalamkari hanging.', 'The range 1250–1950 describes the exhibition, not a date securely assignable to this detail.')
D('8:3.1', 'The Interwoven Globe entrance banner is one of the historic textiles exhibited inside the gallery.', 'The decorative grid is an exhibition design; it is not one historical patchwork textile.')
D('8:3.2', 'The Interwoven Globe arrangement has significance only because the objects are neatly displayed.', 'The hanging, vase-flower textile and framed crowned figures are placed in a shared sightline. Their spatial relationship creates a cross-object comparison of textile, status and imagery.')
D('8:4.1', 'Asia in Amsterdam displays goods and paintings independently, without using their placement to connect imports and consumption.', 'The gallery places imported goods near paintings of luxury consumption, using cases, wall hangings and sightlines to make viewers compare them. Their physical relation supplies the trade argument.')
D('8:6.1', 'The Mumbai graphic advertises the same venue and the same collection range as the Ahmedabad poster.', 'This is a different venue-specific graphic from the Ahmedabad poster, whose range ends in 1950; do not silently merge the two events.')
D('8:7.1', 'The ROM photograph records the textiles in their original historical setting rather than a modern arrangement.', 'The photograph’s historical exhibition theme does not mean every garment in the India Today case is an early-modern original.')
D('8:8.1', 'A Ramayana subject establishes that the ceremonial textile was used only within India.', 'Made in India does not mean made only for Indian use.')
D('8:9.1', 'The Belitung display places identical bowls in neutral storage rows, with no representation of sea travel.', 'The bowls rise in undulating rows around a dhow, turning repeated cargo into a wave-like environment. The arrangement links production scale and sea transport through its specific form.')
D('8:11.1', 'The underwater video still is the museum’s dhow-and-wave arrangement photographed from another angle.', 'The video shows ceramics in an underwater setting. The dhow, curved rows and support rods belong to the separate museum display, a later stage in presenting maritime objects.')
D('8:13.1', 'The reclining beach figure is a sculpture of Caravaggio’s Sleeping Cupid by the same artist.', 'Do not identify artist, title, refugee symbolism or an exact event from this uncaptioned image alone.')
D('8:13.2', 'The paper boats below Sleeping Cupid are depicted inside Caravaggio’s painting.', 'The paper boats on the floor are not depicted inside Caravaggio’s 1608 painting.')
D('8:13.3', 'The Lampedusa display’s argument is fully captured by saying that a painting and boats are in the same room.', 'The painting is elevated above paper boats on the floor, with a barrier in front. That vertical separation and viewing position make the relationship specific.')
D('8:9.2', 'The Cross presents the ocean only as an image depicted on its surface, without material participation.', 'Shell-like accretions cover the cross’s surface and alter its texture. Course reading: the ocean participates materially in the making discussion, while the artist selects and presents the recognizable cross form.')
D('8:16.1', 'The Fort Kochi opening poster’s photographer is necessarily the poster’s designer.', 'A photographer’s credit identifies the documentation, not necessarily the poster designer.')
D('8:16.2', 'The repeated It’s my Biennale posters are different artworks merely because they occupy two wall positions.', 'The top and bottom posters repeat the same portrait design. Two placements document circulation of one design rather than two different image identities.')
D('8:16.3', 'The middle campaign portrait is the same design as the matching top and bottom posters.', 'The middle poster uses a different person’s portrait from the matching top and bottom posters. The slogan connects the campaign while the image variant remains distinct.')
D('8:17.1', 'The Ship of Tarshish is the operating container ship photographed at Vallarpadam.', 'Raghavan’s work is an installation, while the Vallarpadam image documents an operating container ship. Their comparison links artistic representation with contemporary shipping infrastructure.')
D('8:18.1', 'Tallur’s figure with a log and Veni, vidi, vici are two views of one installation.', 'The slide does not title this work Veni, vidi, vici; that caption belongs to the tile installation at right.')
D('8:18.2', 'The tile installation’s meaning is exhausted by arranging terracotta attractively in a hall.', 'The two sloped banks create a bodily passage and bring Mangalore tile manufacture, mission-industry history and architectural material into a contemporary encounter.')
D('8:19.1', 'The framed elephant image is a live camera feed forming part of Tallur’s video screen.', 'The elephant is a separately framed historical-looking image beside the installation. The four-part screen belongs to a different component; the frame is not evidence of a live feed.')
D('8:19.2', 'Quintessential contains only a video, so its sculptural objects and their placement have no interpretive role.', 'The screen is arranged with busts, floor blocks and a smaller framed image. Their spatial relations connect video and sculptural matter within the colonial-legacy discussion.')
D('vallarpadam-container-terminal-lecture-photograph', 'The Vallarpadam photograph documents Raghavan’s gallery installation rather than an actual container ship.', 'The actual container ship and Raghavan’s installation are separate objects, not two views of the same vessel.')
D('oceanic-history-and-connected-art-histories', 'Connected history means every object followed one identical and harmonious trade route.', 'Connected history does not mean every object has an identical route or that cultural contact was always harmonious.')
D('conceptual-authorship-and-collaboration-with-the-ocean', 'Calling the ocean a collaborator removes the artist’s decisions about form, selection and presentation.', 'Material accretion and environmental change can participate alongside the artist’s choice of form, duration, selection and display. Collaboration distributes agency rather than erasing artistic decisions.')
D('kochi-muziris-biennale-and-reclaiming-global-ties', 'Reclaiming global ties proves that Kochi’s ancient trade routes continued unchanged into the present.', 'Reclaiming global ties is a curatorial-historical argument, not proof that every ancient maritime connection remained uninterrupted.')
D('why-reclaim-oceanic-ties-today', 'The final warrior, ascetic and Père Tanguy recap demonstrates one unchanged motif in three identical cultural settings.', 'The recap does not erase differences between an architectural sculpture, a marbled-paper painting and a European portrait.')
